## Finding

**`crime_00022` — Sync I/O in Hot Path** in `src/api/files.ts` (lines 12–15), inside `loadPlanSchema`.

Evidence, verbatim:

> - `` `existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `` `statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `` `readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `lines: 12, 13, 15`
> - `` swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it ``

## The blocking calls

Three `fs` sync methods fire per request: **`existsSync`**, **`statSync`**, **`readFileSync`**. Each one parks the Node event loop on a kernel filesystem syscall — under concurrent load every other in-flight request waits behind them, which is exactly the p99 / event-loop-lag signature described.

(Note: the scan also flags `crime_00008` — that path is a hardcoded `/Users/andrew/...` literal at L9, so `loadPlanSchema` is *also* reading from a path that only exists on one machine. Fix the blocking I/O first; the path bug is a separate problem.)

## Smallest async migration

Goal: same inputs, same return value, just `Promise`-wrapped. Mark `loadPlanSchema` `async`, swap to `fs/promises`, and collapse the `existsSync` + `statSync` precheck into a single `stat` (the `exists` check is redundant — `stat` throws `ENOENT` if missing, and the TOCTOU gap between `existsSync` and `readFileSync` is itself a latent bug).

```ts
// before
import { existsSync, statSync, readFileSync } from "node:fs";

export function loadPlanSchema(): PlanSchema {
  const path = SCHEMA_PATH;
  if (!existsSync(path)) throw new Error(`schema missing: ${path}`);
  const stat = statSync(path);
  if (stat.size === 0) throw new Error(`schema empty: ${path}`);
  const raw = readFileSync(path, "utf8");
  return JSON.parse(raw);
}

// after
import { stat, readFile } from "node:fs/promises";

export async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<PlanSchema> {
  const path = SCHEMA_PATH;
  try {
    const s = await stat(path);
    if (s.size === 0) throw new Error(`schema empty: ${path}`);
  } catch (err: any) {
    if (err?.code === "ENOENT") throw new Error(`schema missing: ${path}`);
    throw err;
  }
  const raw = await readFile(path, "utf8");
  return JSON.parse(raw);
}
```

The return type strictly widens from `T` to `Promise<T>`; argument shape and the resolved value are unchanged. Callers add a single `await` (or `.then`). No caching, no in-memory shim — the event loop stops blocking because the I/O is finally async.
